
  <section xml:id="Math2000Day13">
    <title>Family Friendly Matrices</title>

    <subsection xml:id="games">
      <title>Games</title>
      <p>
        The games are good practice for the geometry question:
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><url href="https://alexblackmath.github.io/games/lineartransformations.html">Linear Transformations Game</url></li>
          <li><url href="https://alexblackmath.github.io/games/HowLoud.html">Area Game</url></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="matrix-notation">
      <title>Matrix Notation</title>
      <p>
        Given a matrix <m>A</m>, we need a way to specify a given entry.
        I use the notation <m>A_{i,j}</m> for the entry in the <m>i</m>th row and <m>j</m>th column.
      </p>
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \end{pmatrix}</me>
            and
            <me>B = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 1 \\ 1 &amp; 0 \\ 1 &amp; 1 \end{pmatrix}</me>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task><p>What is <m>A_{2,3}</m>?</p></task>
        <task><p>What is <m>B_{1,2}</m>?</p></task>
        <task><p>Can you do the multiplication <m>C = AB</m>? If so, what are the dimensions of <m>C</m>? What is <m>C_{1,2}</m>?</p></task>
        <task><p>Can you do the multiplication <m>D = BA</m>? If so, what are the dimensions of <m>D</m>? What is <m>D_{1,2}</m>?</p></task>
      </exercise>
      <p>
        I built a Khan Academy-esque tool to practice this kind of thing with infinite examples.
        If it's something that you find confusing, I encourage you to try it:
        <url href="https://alexblackmath.github.io/games/MatrixNotationGame.html">Matrix Notation Game</url>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="diagonal-matrices">
      <title>Diagonal Matrices</title>
      <p>For the remainder today, I want to spend a great deal of time on specific families of matrices.</p>
      <p>
        A matrix <m>A</m> is called a <term>diagonal</term> matrix if <m>A_{i,j} = 0</m> for all <m>i \neq j</m>.
        That is, everything other than the diagonal from top left to bottom right is <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            A <m>2 \times 2</m> diagonal matrix is always of the form
            <me>\begin{pmatrix} a &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; b \end{pmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task><p>Explain why if <m>a = 0</m> or <m>b = 0</m>, the determinant is <m>0</m>.</p></task>
        <task><p>Explain why if <m>a \neq 0</m> and <m>b \neq 0</m>, the determinant is <m>ab</m>.</p></task>
        <task><p>Find a general expression for the inverse of the matrix when <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are nonzero.</p></task>
      </exercise>
      <p>This is a general fact.</p>
      <theorem>
        <statement><p>The determinant of a diagonal matrix is the product of its diagonal entries.</p></statement>
      </theorem>
      <exercise>
        <p>
          Find the determinant of
          <me>\begin{pmatrix} 2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 3 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 4 \end{pmatrix}.</me>
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <p>There is also an analogous expression for the inverse.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="upper-triangular-matrices">
      <title>Upper Triangular Matrices</title>
      <p>An <term>upper triangular matrix</term> is a matrix where every entry strictly below the diagonal is equal to <m>0</m>.</p>
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            A <m>2 \times 2</m> upper triangular matrix is always of the form
            <me>\begin{pmatrix} a &amp; c \\ 0 &amp; b \end{pmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task><p>Explain why if <m>a = 0</m> or <m>b = 0</m>, the determinant is <m>0</m>.</p></task>
        <task><p>Explain why if <m>a \neq 0</m> and <m>b \neq 0</m>, the determinant is <m>ab</m>.</p></task>
        <task><p>Find a general expression for the inverse of the matrix when <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are nonzero.</p></task>
      </exercise>
      <p>This also generalizes to any upper triangular matrix:</p>
      <theorem>
        <statement><p>The determinant of an upper triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal entries.</p></statement>
      </theorem>
      <p>Finding the inverse is more work. Row reduction is a safer bet than an explicit formula.</p>
      <p>
        There is also a notion of a <term>lower triangular matrix</term>, where every entry
        <alert>strictly</alert> above the diagonal is equal to <m>0</m>. These are precisely the
        transposes of upper triangular matrices. A general, useful fact is the following:
      </p>
      <theorem>
        <statement><p>For any square matrix <m>A</m>, the determinant of <m>A</m> is equal to the determinant of <m>A^{\intercal}</m>.</p></statement>
      </theorem>
      <exercise>
        <p>Explain why the determinant of a lower triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal elements.</p>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="permutation-matrices">
      <title>Permutation Matrices</title>
      <p>
        A matrix is called a <term>permutation matrix</term> if it has precisely one entry
        equal to <m>1</m> in each row and column, and all its other entries are equal to <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <exercise>
        <introduction><p>Do the following:</p></introduction>
        <task><p>Write down all <m>2 \times 2</m> permutation matrices.</p></task>
        <task><p>Find their determinants, and find inverses for the ones that are invertible.</p></task>
        <task><p>Write down all <m>3 \times 3</m> permutation matrices.</p></task>
        <task><p>Find their determinants, and find inverses for the ones that are invertible.</p></task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Permutation matrices are precisely those obtained from the identity matrix
            by only applying interchanges. Consider the following consequences of this fact:
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task><p>Explain why the determinant of a permutation matrix is always equal to <m>1</m> or <m>-1</m>.</p></task>
        <task><p>Explain why all permutation matrices are invertible.</p></task>
        <task><p>Explain why the inverse of a permutation matrix is always a permutation matrix.</p></task>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="simplest-linear-transformations">
      <title>Simplest Linear Transformations</title>
      <p>
        One way to think of a linear transformation is as a function whose coordinates
        are linear combinations of variables. For example, you could get
        <m>T\colon \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{4}</m>:
        <me>T(x,y) = (x+y, x-y, 2x+3y, 4x+10y)</me>
        Each coordinate is a linear combination of the variables, so I can see it's a linear transformation.
      </p>
      <exercise>
        <p>
          Find the matrix for the linear transformation:
          <me>T(x,y) = (x+y, x-y, 2x+3y, 4x+10y).</me>
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <p>
        One special case is when the output in each coordinate is a variable.
        For example, <m>T\colon \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{6}</m> given by
        <me>T(x,y) = (x,y,x,y,x,y)</me>
      </p>
      <exercise>
        <p>
          Find the matrix for the linear transformation
          <me>T(x,y) = (x,y,x,y,x,y).</me>
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <p>
          Find all linear transformations <m>T\colon \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{2}</m>
          such that their output is a variable in each coordinate.
          Write down their corresponding matrices.
        </p>
      </exercise>
      <p>The linear transformations for permutation matrices are of this form.</p>
      <exercise>
        <p>
          Explain why the linear transformation for a permutation matrix is given by
          a single variable in each coordinate. Try to describe in words what these linear transformations do.
        </p>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="general-linear-group">
      <title>General Linear Group</title>
      <p>
        The set of invertible <m>n \times n</m> matrices is called the
        <term>General Linear Group</term> and denoted <m>\mathrm{GL}_{n}</m>.
      </p>
      <exercise>
        <p>Find an explicit expression for the inverse of an invertible <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix.</p>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="special-linear-group">
      <title>Special Linear Group</title>
      <p>
        The special linear group <m>\mathrm{SL}_{n}</m> is the collection of
        <m>n \times n</m> matrices of determinant <m>1</m>.
      </p>
      <exercise>
        <introduction><p>Which of the following operations preserve membership in the special linear group?</p></introduction>
        <task><p>Replacing the first row <m>R_{1}</m> by it minus twice the second row.</p></task>
        <task><p>Rescaling row <m>1</m> by a factor of <m>2</m>.</p></task>
        <task><p>For a <m>4 \times 4</m> matrix, interchanging row <m>1</m> and row <m>2</m> as well as row <m>3</m> and row <m>4</m>.</p></task>
      </exercise>
      <p>
        While a fair bit trickier to prove, it turns out that <m>\mathrm{SL}_{n}</m>
        is the collection of matrices obtained from the identity matrix by applying only row replacements.
        Try to show this for <m>\mathrm{SL}_{2}</m>:
      </p>
      <exercise>
        <introduction><p>Let's consider the <m>2 \times 2</m> case:</p></introduction>
        <task><p>Which diagonal matrices have determinant <m>1</m>?</p></task>
        <task><p>Which upper triangular matrices have determinant <m>1</m>?</p></task>
        <task><p>Explain why any <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix is either upper triangular or can be made upper triangular by only applying replacements.</p></task>
        <task><p>Explain why any invertible upper triangular matrix that is not diagonal can be made diagonal with one replacement.</p></task>
        <task><p>Describe which diagonal matrices have determinant <m>1</m>.</p></task>
        <task><p>Show how to go from a diagonal matrix with determinant <m>1</m> to the identity matrix by only applying replacements.</p></task>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>
